TOUR=L('Albany Heritage Area walking-tour brochure (Discover Albany)','https://res.cloudinary.com/simpleview/image/upload/v1/clients/albany/WalkingTourBrochure_web_FINAL_615a42e0-1b41-40e7-bb60-2eafd998f33e.pdf')
PLACES=[
N('st-josephs-church-albany','St. Joseph\'s Church (Ten Broeck Street)','Albany','alb','landmark','standing',42.65694,-73.75146,'exact','view','walk','','1855–1860',
  'A Gothic Revival Roman Catholic church by Patrick Keely, who also designed the Cathedral of the Immaculate Conception; ground was broken in 1855 and it was finished in 1860. It was last used for worship in the 1990s and then passed through owners whose plans came to nothing. The city took it in 2003 and gave it to Historic Albany Foundation, which spent close to a million dollars stabilizing it between 2001 and 2012 and opened it for concerts, art shows and house tours; it went back to the city in 2012. The city put it up for sale in 2023 for $100,000, and the foundation lists it among Albany\'s "Places in Peril".',
  'A tall blue limestone church trimmed with Ohio sandstone on the hill of the Ten Broeck Triangle, a landmark of the north Albany skyline. Wikipedia counts 14 marble columns and 16 stained glass windows inside; the foundation says the last pews were taken out and sold after 2012. The interior is closed; see it from Ten Broeck Street and the streets round the triangle.',
  'Patrick Keely\'s best church, empty and for sale for a hundred thousand dollars.',
  'Owned by the City of Albany and closed (for sale since February 2023, per the foundation and Wikipedia). View only from the public streets. Street parking in the neighbourhood; the nearest mapped lots are downtown, about 500 m south (Kennedy Garage on Van Tromp Street).',
  [HAF,L('The Evangelist, July 6, 2023: a part of historic Albany','https://evangelist.org/news/2023/jul/06/a-part-of-historic-albany/'),W('Saint Joseph\'s Church (Albany, New York)')],
  brick='no'),
N('st-johns-church-green-street-albany','St. John\'s Church (Green Street), derelict','Albany','alb','landmark','standing',42.64308,-73.7533,'exact','view','walk','','1903–1908',
  'A former Roman Catholic church in the Pastures, built 1903–08 to designs by Charles Ogden. It was planned taller and larger but cut down as the congregation was already shrinking. It is one of Albany\'s earliest steel-framed buildings, which is why it is still standing after more than 40 years empty and a fire. Several plans (apartments, a grocery, a study centre) came to nothing. Historic Albany Foundation lists it among its "Places in Peril".',
  'A stone-clad church on Green Street whose roof holes can be seen from Interstate 787, the foundation says. Inside, it reports, the finishes are badly decayed and the floors are giving way. Look from Green Street only.',
  'A steel skeleton dressed as a church, still upright after forty years empty.',
  'Privately owned (a development company, per the foundation\'s 2024 page) and derelict: unsafe and closed. View only from Green Street. The nearest mapped lot is the state\'s Pastures lot on Madison Avenue, about 130 m north-east (OpenStreetMap; who may park there was not checked).',
  [HAF],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('holy-innocents-church-albany','Church of the Holy Innocents','Albany','alb','landmark','standing',42.65886,-73.74835,'exact','view','walk','','1849–1850',
  'An early Gothic Revival Episcopal church and chapel by Frank Wills, built on land given by William De Witt in memory of his children who had died (the foundation dates it 1849, Wikipedia 1850). About a century later the congregation moved and sold it to a Russian Orthodox church, which added the green onion dome. In the 1980s it became a private house, with the chapel as the living quarters and the church as the owner\'s library. It has been empty since the late 1990s; part of the rear fell in 2015, a developer bought it in 2016, and the foundation lists it among its "Places in Peril". A city plan for the Clinton Square area proposes keeping it as an open-air arts venue and park.',
  'Two T-shaped buildings of coursed rubble stone with slate roofs, joined by a short link, in a small overgrown garden at North Pearl and Colonie Streets; much of it was covered in ivy when Wikipedia\'s article was written. The foundation says the Bolton Brothers stained glass and part of the painted starry ceiling survive inside, and that there is a hole in the sanctuary roof. Closed; see it from the street.',
  'A country-parish church with an onion dome, empty for twenty-five years in north Albany.',
  'Privately owned (an LLC, per the foundation\'s 2024 page) and closed. View from North Pearl Street and Colonie Street. Nearest mapped parking: the Capital Repertory Theatre lot on North Pearl Street, about 150 m south, or Kirk Plaza (OpenStreetMap; terms not checked).',
  [HAF,W('Church of the Holy Innocents (Albany, New York)')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('albany-public-bath-2','Public Bath House No. 2','Albany','alb','civic','standing',42.63887,-73.75771,'exact','view','walk','','1905',
  'One of three public bath houses the city built at the height of the public-health movement, when few homes had indoor plumbing; designed in the City Engineer\'s office by William Obenaus in 1905. People from several neighbourhoods and nearby towns used it until 2010, when the city closed it over the cost of running and repairing it. The city has offered it for sale since 2024; WAMC reported in November 2025 that nobody had asked about buying it and that the city was asking for proposals again.',
  'A yellow brick building on Fourth Avenue in the South End with vines up its walls and a decaying wooden entrance ramp (WAMC, November 2025). The foundation calls it in fair condition apart from a roof leak. Closed; see it from the street. (The federal place-names file wrongly lists "Public Bath Number 2" as vanished.)',
  'A 1905 public bath house from before indoor plumbing, closed since 2010 and unsold.',
  'Owned by the City of Albany and closed (for sale). View from Fourth Avenue. A small mapped lot on South Pearl Street is about 100 m south-west (OpenStreetMap; terms not checked); otherwise street parking.',
  [HAF,L('WAMC, Nov. 25, 2025: Public Bathhouse No. 2 for sale again','https://www.wamc.org/2025-11-25/public-bathhouse-no-2-for-sale-albany-tries-again-to-sell-fourth-avenue-property')],
  brick='no'),
N('james-hall-office-albany','James Hall Office, Lincoln Park','Albany','alb','inst','standing',42.64583,-73.76861,'site','view','walk','','1852',
  'A small Italianate brick office and laboratory designed in 1852 by Andrew Jackson Downing and Calvert Vaux, one of the few buildings left from their short partnership, for James Hall, New York\'s first state geologist and paleontologist. Hall did much of his work in this building, his office and laboratory; among other things he showed that the stromatolites of the Petrified Sea Gardens near Saratoga Springs were once living. It is a National Historic Landmark (1976). A school addition was built on in 1977; the building has been empty since 2011, was sold in April 2024 with the former Sunshine School, and the foundation lists it among its "Places in Peril".',
  'A one-story brick building, painted white with red brick showing through in the 2008 Wikipedia photograph, with a low front gable and a small square tower at one corner, joined along its east side to a later school building, at the western edge of Lincoln Park. Closed; interior not checked. See it from the park.',
  'Where New York\'s first state geologist worked out the fossil record, in a Downing and Vaux villa.',
  'Privately owned (sold April 2024, per the foundation) and closed. Lincoln Park round it is a city park; see the building from the park paths. Street parking on the park\'s edges; a lot OpenStreetMap maps on Park Avenue, about 300 m north-east, could not be made out on the aerial. Park hours not checked.',
  [HAF,W('James Hall Office')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('erie-canal-lock-one-albany','Erie Canal Lock One (old Lock 1)','Albany','alb','canal','traces',42.65722,-73.74284,'site','view','walk','canal','1823–1825',
  'The first lock of the Erie Canal, where boats came up from the Hudson into the canal; the original lock dates from 1823–25 and was later rebuilt as an enlarged lock. After the Barge Canal replaced the old canal on a different route it was lost: the Delaware and Hudson Railroad filled the ground round it and used the lock as a dump, and it lay buried for about a century. In October 2002 two Union College professors found and uncovered parts of it. In August 2026 a SUNY Polytechnic professor and an archaeologist dug out the eastern part again for a proposed marina and plaza that would restore the lock (WAMC and Spectrum News, August 2026).',
  'The site lies between the Central Warehouse and I-787 in the warehouse district. Whether the 2026 excavation was left open, fenced or filled again was not checked. A two-sided Canal Society marker about the Albany Basin and Lock 1 stands by the river in the Corning Preserve.',
  'The first lock of the Erie Canal, dug out again after a hundred years.',
  'Who owns the ground was not checked: treat it as view-only from the public streets. The state\'s Water Street Annex lot is about 200 m south-west (OpenStreetMap; terms not checked).',
  [L('WAMC, Aug. 17, 2026: Erie Canal\'s old Lock One excavated','https://www.wamc.org/news/2026-08-17/albany-erie-canal-lock-one-excavation-development-project'),L('Spectrum News, Aug. 24, 2026: unearthing a piece of Erie Canal history','https://spectrumlocalnews.com/nys/capital-region/news/2026/08/24/unearthing-a-piece-of-erie-canal-history-'),L('HMdb: The Erie Canal / Albany Basin marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=5174')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('quackenbush-house-albany','Quackenbush House','Albany','alb','landmark','standing',42.65402,-73.74846,'exact','public','walk','','1730s',
  'A Dutch brick house with a steep double-pitched gable roof, named for the family that lived in it for about 150 years. Wikipedia dates it to about 1736; the heritage-area brochure says the front part, toward Broadway, is Dutch work of the 1730s, perhaps of bricks made at a brickyard on the site, and the rear part Federal, of the late 1700s. Long thought the oldest building in Albany, it is now known to be second to 48 Hudson Avenue. It stood outside the city line until Albany annexed the area in 1812.',
  'The Dutch front with its steep gable faces Broadway in Quackenbush Square, beside the old pumping station and the visitors center. It has been a restaurant since 1995; Wikipedia names an English-style pub as the current tenant (not checked). Interior not checked.',
  'Albany\'s second-oldest building, a 1730s Dutch house that is now a pub.',
  'A restaurant in Quackenbush Square (who owns the building was not checked); the inside is open when the restaurant is (hours not checked). The Quackenbush Square Garage on Orange Street is about 100 m west (OpenStreetMap).',
  [W('Quackenbush House'),TOUR],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes',th=[42.65349,-73.74758],thn='Quackenbush Square Garage'),
N('albany-pump-station','Quackenbush Pumping Station (Albany Pump Station)','Albany','alb','water','standing',42.65413,-73.74777,'exact','public','walk','','1873',
  'The city bought the corner of Montgomery and Quackenbush Streets in 1873 and built this brick pumping station to Edward Ogden\'s design the same year, with two steam pumps sending Hudson River water up to Bleecker Reservoir (now Bleecker Stadium, already in the Atlas). It was enlarged later in the century and pumped for about 60 years. In the 1980s Quackenbush Square was renovated: part of the station and a neighbouring 1852 townhouse became the Albany Heritage Area Visitors Center, and the rest a brewpub, which won an award from the Preservation League of New York State.',
  'A two-story brick main block with a slate hip roof, and a former stable with round-arched windows to the south. The brewpub kept the industrial interior and two 20-ton cranes, now used to lift its serving tanks (Wikipedia).',
  'The waterworks that fed Bleecker Reservoir, now a brewpub that kept its cranes.',
  'Brewpub and the Albany Heritage Area Visitors Center (Quackenbush Square; the brochure gives the visitors center\'s address as 25 Quackenbush Square). Who owns the building was not checked; the inside is open when they are (hours not checked). The Quackenbush Square Garage is next to it (OpenStreetMap).',
  [W('Albany Pump Station'),TOUR],
  brick='yes',th=[42.65349,-73.74758],thn='Quackenbush Square Garage'),
N('van-ostrande-radliff-house','Van Ostrande–Radliff House (48 Hudson Avenue)','Albany','alb','landmark','standing',42.64753,-73.75188,'exact','public','walk','','1728',
  'The oldest building in Albany. Johannes van Ostrande built it in 1728 just outside the stockade; a tree-ring sample tested at Columbia University\'s Lamont–Doherty laboratory confirmed the date. It was earlier thought to date from 1759. In the 1750s he sold it to Johannes Radliff, a shoemaker; in the 1800s it became a house and then a leather-goods works, and from the 1930s Saul\'s Equipment sold kitchenware here until 1996, when it fell empty. Historic Albany Foundation restored it from 2020.',
  'A small Dutch house with a brick first floor, hidden among later additions. Wikipedia lists a molded anchor beam, the outline of a jambless fireplace, wide pine siding and floorboards, steep roof beams and original brick walls. Open to the public on First Fridays, February to October, 5:30 to 7 p.m., free, with short guided tours at 5:30, 6 and 6:30 (foundation event page, 2026); from spring 2026 the foundation also holds monthly workshops and lectures here.',
  'Albany\'s oldest building, dated by tree rings to 1728.',
  'Owned and restored by Historic Albany Foundation. Inside only at its open houses and events (First Fridays, February to October, 2026 schedule). Otherwise see it from Hudson Avenue. Street parking or the downtown garages.',
  [W('48 Hudson Avenue'),L('Historic Albany Foundation: First Friday open house and tours (2026)','https://www.historic-albany.org/calendar/2026/4/3/van-ostrande-radliff-house-first-friday-open-house-amp-tours')],
  brick='?'),
N('cathedral-immaculate-conception-albany','Cathedral of the Immaculate Conception','Albany','alb','landmark','standing',42.6475,-73.75972,'exact','public','walk','','1848–1852',
  'The mother church of the Roman Catholic Diocese of Albany, designed by Patrick Keely and built 1848–52; the south tower was not finished until 1902. It is the second-oldest cathedral in New York State and the first American Catholic cathedral in the Gothic Revival style, and when finished it was the tallest building in Albany. It survived the clearing of the surrounding neighbourhood for the Empire State Plaza in the 1960s, and was restored inside and out in the 2000s after its roof began leaking.',
  'Brick walls faced in sandstone, with twin towers. Inside are the original stained glass windows of the nave, made in Birmingham, England, the Lady window in the chancel, a south-transept Last Judgment window of 1897, choir stalls carved in Belgium in 1894, a Dutch oak pulpit, a Caen stone font and award-winning Stations of the Cross; the restoration brought back the original colours (Wikipedia).',
  'Albany\'s Catholic cathedral, kept when the Empire State Plaza swept the neighbourhood away.',
  'The cathedral of the Diocese of Albany, 125 Eagle Street at Madison Avenue. Sunday Mass at 11 a.m. and 5 p.m. (cathedral page, November 2026); tours by arrangement with the office (518-463-4447). Parking in the museum lot behind the cathedral on weekends, state holidays and weekdays after 4 p.m. (cathedral directions page).',
  [L('Cathedral of the Immaculate Conception: Mass schedule','https://www.cathedralic.com/mass-schedule'),L('Cathedral: directions and parking','https://www.cathedralic.com/directions'),L('Cathedral: tours','https://www.cathedralic.com/tour'),W('Cathedral of the Immaculate Conception (Albany, New York)')],
  brick='no'),
N('cathedral-all-saints-albany','Cathedral of All Saints','Albany','alb','landmark','standing',42.65444,-73.75806,'exact','public','walk','','1884',
  'The Episcopal cathedral of Albany, designed by the young Robert W. Gibson, who won the commission over H. H. Richardson. The cornerstone was laid in 1884 (Wikipedia also says construction began in 1888) and it was the first Episcopal church in the United States built as a cathedral. It was never finished: the planned spires were not built, and the State Education Building took the rest of the block, though some foundations for the planned parts remain. The west front was finished only in 1971.',
  'Potsdam sandstone with brownstone trim; a brick chapter house stands at the south-east corner. Inside: John LaFarge\'s rose window in the west wall, wall windows by Clayton and Bell of London, stone carving by Louis Hinton finished in 1891, an iron and brass rood screen, mosaic floors in the choir and side chapels, a polychrome reredos with statues, and choir stalls salvaged from a 17th-century church in Bruges (Wikipedia).',
  'An unfinished Gothic cathedral with choir stalls from Bruges.',
  'Episcopal Diocese of Albany, corner of South Swan and Elk Streets. Weekday services at 7:30 a.m., 12:05 p.m. (Lady Chapel) and 5 p.m.; Sunday Eucharist at 8 and 10 a.m. (cathedral page, November 2026). The heritage-area page says entry is free, the church is open weekdays round the noon service, and tours are given at events and by appointment. Weekday parking in the alley is by permit only; use paid city parking.',
  [L('Cathedral of All Saints (services, parking)','https://www.cathedralofallsaints.org'),L('Hudson River Valley National Heritage Area: Cathedral of All Saints','https://www.hudsonrivervalley.com/sites/Cathedral-of-All-Saints/details'),W('Cathedral of All Saints (Albany, New York)')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('st-peters-episcopal-albany','St. Peter\'s Church (Episcopal)','Albany','alb','landmark','standing',42.65083,-73.75389,'exact','public','walk','','1860–1876',
  'Albany\'s Anglican congregation dates from services for British soldiers in 1708. The present church, by Richard Upjohn with the tower by his son, was finished in 1860 and the tower in 1876; it is counted among the elder Upjohn\'s best works. Lord Howe, killed at the Battle of Ticonderoga in 1758, is buried here; Wikipedia calls him the only British peer buried in the United States.',
  'Walls of Schenectady bluestone with New Jersey sandstone trim and a square tower with three large gargoyles. Inside are original Clayton and Bell windows, sculpture by Louis Saint-Gaudens, and a Tiffany rose window over the State Street entrance (Wikipedia and the heritage-area brochure).',
  'An Upjohn church on State Street with a Tiffany rose window and a British lord in its crypt.',
  'An active Episcopal parish at 107 State Street. Open for prayer Monday to Friday, 11:30 a.m. to 1:30 p.m. (parish page, archived copy of August 7, 2026; the live site did not answer). A mapped lot on Corning Place is about 70 m north-west; otherwise downtown garages.',
  [L('St. Peter\'s Church, Albany','https://stpeterschurchalbany.org'),TOUR,W('St. Peter\'s Episcopal Church (Albany, New York)')],
  brick='no'),
N('st-marys-church-albany','St. Mary\'s Church (Lodge Street)','Albany','alb','landmark','standing',42.65199,-73.75272,'exact','public','walk','','1869',
  'The third church of the oldest Catholic congregation in upstate New York, founded in 1796; the parish once covered most of the state. This church was dedicated in 1869. The statue of the Angel Gabriel (Wikipedia: the Angel of Judgment) blowing a trumpet on the 175-foot steeple has been a city landmark since. Wikipedia repeats parish lore that Lafayette heard Mass in the earlier church in 1824–25, but says no newspaper of the time records it.',
  'An Italian Romanesque Revival brick church with marble trim on a bluestone foundation. Inside: ceiling and wall paintings by Italian artists of 1891–95 (restored 1982), stained glass of 1912, and Stations of the Cross bought from a German church (Wikipedia, heritage-area brochure).',
  'Upstate New York\'s oldest Catholic parish, under a trumpeting angel.',
  'An active Catholic church at 10 Lodge Street. The cathedral\'s page says the downtown weekday Eucharist is held here at 12:05 p.m., Monday to Friday (since January 2024). The Hotel Albany Garage on Lodge Street is about 90 m south.',
  [W('St. Mary\'s Church (Albany, New York)'),TOUR,L('Cathedral of the Immaculate Conception: daily Eucharist at St. Mary\'s','https://www.cathedralic.com')],
  brick='yes'),
N('albany-institute-history-art','Albany Institute of History & Art','Albany','alb','inst','standing',42.65582,-73.76028,'exact','public','walk','','1791',
  'Founded in 1791, one of the oldest museums in the United States; its natural-history collections from the state surveys became the start of the New York State Museum. It is housed in two connected buildings, the older Rice Building by Richard Morris Hunt and a later brick main building, joined by a modern glass entrance built in the early 2000s.',
  'A museum of the history, art and culture of Albany and the upper Hudson. The Rice Building has golden Roman brick walls on a raised stone base; the main building is two-story brick with quoins and a limestone belt course (Wikipedia); both are buff and yellow brick in the 2011 photograph.',
  '',
  'Open Wednesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. and Sunday noon to 5 p.m., closed Monday and Tuesday; adults $12, seniors and students $10, children 6–12 $8 (museum page, November 2026). Free parking for visitors in the museum lot at Dove and Elk Streets.',
  [L('Albany Institute: visit','https://www.albanyinstitute.org/visit'),W('Albany Institute of History & Art')],
  brick='no'),
N('palace-theatre-albany','Palace Theatre','Albany','alb','amuse','standing',42.65498,-73.75029,'exact','public','walk','','1930–1931',
  'An RKO movie palace by the theatre architect John Eberson, built from June 1930 and opened in October 1931 with a stage for vaudeville between films; it was the city\'s largest movie house. After it closed the City of Albany bought it for $90,000 and reopened it. A $3 million restoration finished in January 2003 brought the interior back toward its 1931 look and replicated the original marquee. It holds over 150 events a year.',
  'A tan brick and stone front at Clinton Avenue and North Pearl Street. Inside: an Austrian Baroque auditorium with heavy plaster ornament, statuary, arched boxes and a Czech crystal chandelier (Wikipedia). Inside during shows and events.',
  '',
  'Owned by the City of Albany and run as a theatre, 19 Clinton Avenue; inside only with a ticket to an event (schedule not checked). The Kennedy Garage on Van Tromp Street is about 260 m south.',
  [W('Palace Theatre (Albany, New York)'),TOUR],
  brick='no'),
]
